//! Measured colour for Sony bodies: a DNG-style two-illuminant calibration
//! per body, computed from measured spectral sensitivities, so an ARW from
//! one of them takes the DNG colour path (`dng::camera_color`) exactly as a
//! DNG does.
//!
//! ## Where the numbers come from
//!
//! `colour_table.rs` is generated by `tests/sony_colour_table.rs` from the
//! Academy Software Foundation's `rawtoaces-data` (Apache-2.0; the CIE 1931
//! observer in it is CC BY 4.0), vendored unmodified in
//! `engine/core/data/rawtoaces/` with its commit. No Adobe data and no
//! other raw converter's table was used. The test recomputes the table and
//! fails if the committed one differs from what the data gives.
//!
//! ## The method
//!
//! For each body and each of two illuminants, L:
//!
//! 1. Responses, summed over 380..780 nm in 5 nm steps: the camera's
//!    `c_i = Σ r_i(λ) L(λ) S(λ)` (S its measured R, G, B sensitivities) and
//!    the CIE 1931 observer's `X_i = Σ r_i(λ) L(λ) x̄ȳz̄(λ)`, for the 190
//!    training reflectances r_i of the dataset and for a perfect white
//!    (`r = 1`), which gives the camera's neutral `w` and the white `X_w`.
//! 2. White balance: `b_i = c_i / w` per channel (the white is 1, 1, 1),
//!    and `y_i = X_i / Y_w` (the white has Y = 1).
//! 3. The camera → XYZ matrix `K` that minimises `Σ_i |y_i − K b_i|²`, the
//!    squared error in XYZ of the training colours, subject to
//!    `K (1, 1, 1) = X_w / Y_w`, so white stays exactly white. Linear least
//!    squares with one equality constraint a row, solved in closed form
//!    (Lagrange). The error is minimised in XYZ because camera → XYZ is
//!    the map a decode applies: it is the error of the decoded colours, in
//!    a space linear in light like the data. A perceptual (CIELAB) fit would
//!    need a non-linear solve; the generator reports the resulting ΔE*ab.
//! 4. The DNG `ColorMatrix` (XYZ → camera): `diag(w / w_G) K⁻¹`.
//!
//! The two illuminants are CIE standard illuminant A, computed from its
//! definition (a Planckian radiator at 2848 K with c2 = 1.435e-2 m·K), and,
//! because the dataset holds no daylight spectrum, a Planckian radiator at
//! 6504 K standing in for D65: the calibration is tagged D65 (EXIF code
//! 21), whose colour temperature it has. The generator's second test bounds
//! what that stand-in can move: the daylight fit, adapted to D50, changes
//! by little between Planckian lights of 5500 K and 7500 K.
//!
//! ## How a decode uses it
//!
//! A body is matched on the exact `Model` string IFD0 carries (no aliases:
//! the dataset maps ILCE-7RM5, ILCE-7RM4A and ILCE-7RM3A to other bodies'
//! data, which is not used). Its calibration has the two matrices, the
//! as-shot neutral from Sony's white-balance multipliers (`1 / gain`), no
//! analog balance, no forward matrices and no baseline exposure.
//! `dng::camera_color` then gives the white point of the as-shot neutral,
//! interpolates the two matrices by inverse colour temperature, inverts,
//! adapts with Bradford to D50 and on to linear sRGB (D65).


use crate::dng::camera_color::{Calibration, DngCalibration};

/// One body's calibration, as a DNG would carry it.
pub struct SonyColour {
    /// The exact `Model` string of IFD0.
    pub model: &'static str,
    /// XYZ → camera under CIE illuminant A, row-major (`ColorMatrix1`).
    pub color_matrix_a: [f64; 9],
    /// XYZ → camera under daylight (D65), row-major (`ColorMatrix2`).
    pub color_matrix_daylight: [f64; 9],
}

/// The measured calibration for `model`, with the as-shot white balance
/// given as channel gains (green 1), or `None` for a body without data.
pub fn calibration_for(model: &str, as_shot_gain: [f32; 3]) -> Option<DngCalibration> {
    let entry = super::colour_table::SONY_COLOUR.iter().find(|c| c.model == model)?;
    let cal = DngCalibration {
        first: Calibration { illuminant: 17, color_matrix: entry.color_matrix_a, forward_matrix: None, camera_calibration: None },
        second: Some(Calibration { illuminant: 21, color_matrix: entry.color_matrix_daylight, forward_matrix: None, camera_calibration: None }),
        analog_balance: [1.0; 3],
        as_shot_neutral: Some(as_shot_gain.map(|g| 1.0 / f64::from(g))),
        as_shot_white_xy: None,
        baseline_exposure: 0.0,
    };
    cal.validate().ok()?;
    Some(cal)
}
